feat(deploy): run the sealer on a scoped store identity, not the store's root (#228)

deploy/compose/innsegl.yml gave innsegl-sealer and innsegl-canary the same
value it gave the server as MINIO_ROOT_USER, so the sealer ran as the object
store's root account for the whole life of the deployment. An identity read
off the running container could therefore set the bucket's object-lock
configuration and downgrade the default rule from COMPLIANCE to GOVERNANCE,
after which every segment written afterwards is deletable by a holder of a
bypass-capable credential.

Sealed history was never at risk and that is why the narrowing is worth
exactly this much: COMPLIANCE retention refuses deletion by anyone, the
account that wrote it included. What is protected here is future writes.

object-init.sh still creates the locked bucket as root, because S3 object lock
can only be enabled at bucket creation and only an account that may set a
bucket configuration can put the default rule on it. That is a one-time setup
step. It now also creates a scoped identity and attaches a policy granting
what the sealer and the canary do - read and write objects and their
retention, list versions, delete versions - and withholding two permissions by
name: s3:PutBucketObjectLockConfiguration and s3:BypassGovernanceRetention.

verify-object-scope.sh is db-init.sh's last line in the other medium. A policy
attached once lives in somebody's deployment, and a later
`mc admin policy attach readwrite --user` leaves no trace in this repository.
So the one-shot ends by asking the server what the credential can really do,
and the sealer gates on the one-shot completing. It writes nothing: the bucket
holds retained objects it could never remove, and the one attempt that could
change anything writes the rule the bucket already has.

Unset, the scoped secret is derived from the root secret rather than required.
An operator upgrading has set a root credential once and never heard of this
issue; a narrowing that needed a new variable before the stack came up would
be switched off rather than adopted.

Driven by OPS-025, OPS-026 and OPS-028, all three observed failing first:
OPS-025 and OPS-028 reported the sealer holding the root credential, and
OPS-026 watched that credential set the bucket's object-lock configuration
while the root identity set it too. OPS-027 passed before and after, which is
the measurement rather than a gap - it now guards the other direction, that
the scope did not buy its refusals by taking away what SEG-005's canary needs
to prove a refusal means anything.

Measured against the live stack: object-init provisioned the identity and its
verifier reported the three refusals; innsegl canary PASS under the scoped
credential, eight checks, against the deployment's own bucket.

